nt who simultaneously exhausts a count dimension AND the dimensional dimension sees the count-dimension Resource surface in ExceededDetail (the LAST Reserve that fires produces the error). Three fakes (noopQuotaReserver in cmd/yalla-api/main.go, nopQuotaReserver in store/internal_test.go, recordingQuota in store/projectservice_test.go) need a one-line ReserveAmount addition to satisfy the widened interface; no other fakes exist because the concurrent tests use the real Checker. Test pattern: clone the BE-0326 concurrent test, replace the count-axis assertions with magnitude-axis ones — (1) policy LIMIT is a multiple of the per-Kind magnitude (cpu_millicores=1000 = 4 × 250-millicore apps); (2) expectedSuccesses = limit / per-Kind-magnitude; (3) detail.Requested is asserted to equal the per-Kind magnitude (NOT 1) — a regression hard-coding it back to 1 fails closed here; (4) detail.Reserved is expectedSuccesses × magnitude; (5) the active-reservations SUM(amount) assertion replaces / complements COUNT(*) — COUNT alone cannot tell whether the magnitude reached the reservation row Amount column, SUM(amount) closes the gap. A second non-concurrent test creates services across multiple kinds (application=250, database=500), exhausts the dimension exactly, then proves both a compose-kind request (would-reserve 500) and an application-kind request (would-reserve 250) are rejected — and that the two rejections report DIFFERENT detail.Requested values. The single equality check `composeDetail.Requested != appDetail.Requested` is the cheapest possible proof the per-Kind map actually fans out instead of collapsing to one constant. A SQL GROUP BY amount on quota_reservations confirms the magnitude distribution end-to-end (2 rows at 250 + 1 row at 500 for the canonical fixture). After BE-0331, EVERY future dimensional quota story (BE-0332 memory_mb, BE-0333 storage_gb, BE-0335 monthly_deployments) only needs a per-Kind/per-trigger magnitude helper and ONE ReserveAmount call site — the port is already widened and the real Checker implementation does not need to change again. The Reserve(...) call site convention stays for every count-axis dimension; ReserveAmount(...) is reserved for dimensions whose amount actually varies per request.
- BE-0332 memory_mb is the SECOND instance of the dimensional-quota template (BE-0331) and confirms the template clones without any port widening, Checker change, or fake update — only (a) a per-Kind magnitude map + helper in serviceservice.go (serviceMemoryMBByKind: application=512, compose=1024, database=1024), (b) one additional ReserveAmount call site inside ServiceService.Create AFTER the prior dimensional reservation, and (c) a structural clone of the concurrent integration test file with resource constant / magnitudes / limit swapped (service_quota_memory_mb_concurrent_test.go). Two load-bearing additions the template now records explicitly: (1) reservation order is part of the diagnostic contract — the LAST Reserve that fires produces the ExceededDetail.Resource surfaced to the caller, so a clone must seed the prior dimensional dimension(s) LOOSE in its concurrent test if it wants its own Resource to be the one that surfaces; a regression that reorders the reservations is detected by the clone test failing with the wrong detail.Resource (cpu_millicores instead of memory_mb, or vice versa). (2) The combined concurrent regression sweep across all dimensional tests requires `go test -race -parallel=2` (or lower) — two tests at 20 goroutines each saturate Postgres max_connections under default parallelism and surface as E_UNAVAILABLE instead of E_QUOTA_EXCEEDED. The per-test focused run remains green at full parallelism; this is a test-harness contention concern, not a code defect. Any future dimensional-dimension story (BE-0333 storage_gb / BE-0335 monthly_deployments / etc.) adds another concurrent test to the family and inherits both rules.
